ity is not.** Two utility share offerings were cut back in size. Pellham Power then withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short.\n- **Pellham is not using debt to make up the shortfall.** It says it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes \"until financing improves.\" Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria, and it had planned 2027 capex up 18%. I found no sign that it plans to replace the equity with bonds. Likely reasons are leverage or credit-rating limits, or the wider spreads, but the sources don't say.\n- **Norvane looks better placed.** Its 2027 capex is planned up 12%, and its bond deal went through without trouble. That suggests it can fund its plan largely with debt.\n\n**Conclusion:** Healthy issuers such as Norvane can probably fund 2027 capex through debt. Pellham apparently can't, or won't, and is cutting spending instead. Debt markets alone will not fully fund the sector's planned 2027 capex.\n\n**Confidence:** moderate. The evidence is three short documents. I found no data on issuer leverage, ratings, or total debt needed. The equity question is covered by n6.","ttft_ms":3817,"type":"result","duration_ms":20292,"uuid":"a2dc0217-a674-4793-b70e-ab6369df5fab","ttft_stream_ms":2621,"time_to_request_ms":116,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":8,"system_prompt":7,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":9,"input_attachments":29,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":19,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":5,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":8,"client_creation":2,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":66,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":72,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":204,"process_anon_mib_tu